#
# The disk pack program at boot: the card mounted, and cadr-disk-packs serving
# the CADR's disks from the drive bay at /mnt/card/packs.
#
# **THE CARD IS ONE FAT32 PARTITION AND IT IS MOUNTED READ-WRITE.**  The user
# formats the card themselves and unpacks a zip onto it, so everything is in
# one filesystem:
#
#   /dev/mmcblk0p1 -> /mnt/card   READ-WRITE.
#       BOOT.BIN, u-boot.img (or u-boot.itb) and uEnv.txt at the root, whose
#       names the loader fixes; README.TXT, `fpgarc` and `muirrc` beside them,
#       which are the files a person edits; `clock` beside those, which THIS
#       script writes at a clean shutdown because the board has no clock of
#       its own; the fabric, the tree, the kernel and the root filesystem in a
#       folder named for the board, which is what the TFTP server's directory
#       for it is called too; the band's Lisp files in `sys/` and `site/`; and
#       the drive bay in `packs/`.
#
#       A pack is what the machine writes, and it is written in place --- a
#       pack has to stay the size the drive is, so there is no temporary file
#       and no rename, as muir's diskpack says of its own writes.  The program
#       syncs every block it writes.
#
# FAT32 is deliberate, so that the card can be read and written from Windows,
# which cannot write ext4.  The price is no journal.
#
# **AND THE LOADER'S FILES ARE NO LONGER BEHIND A READ-ONLY MOUNT.**  They used
# to be: the card had two partitions, the first holding the loader and mounted
# read-only so that the machine's constant writing to a pack could not damage
# it.  That is given up with the second partition, deliberately, and what makes
# it bearable is the remedy.  A boot file damaged by a power cut is repaired by
# unpacking the zip onto the card again; a two-partition image needed the whole
# card rewritten with a tool that destroys the wrong disk if the device name is
# wrong.  Nothing here remounts anything read-only, and a file at the root can
# be replaced from the board with a plain `cp` and a `sync`.
#
# **A CARD OF THE OLD TWO-PARTITION SHAPE STILL WORKS, AND IS TOLD IT IS
# OLD.**  Those cards exist --- this project's own board has one --- and their
# `fpgarc`, `muirrc`, `clock` and flat drive bay are all on the second
# partition.  So the mount below takes the second partition when there is one,
# and the first when there is not, and it says which it found.  It is the one
# place on the board that knows there were ever two shapes: every other init
# script reads `/mnt/card/fpgarc`, which is true either way, and the bay's
# directory reaches the program as `--packs`, which is `/mnt/card/packs` on a
# card of the new shape and `/mnt/card` on a card of the old one.  `mksd-*.sh`
# makes only the new shape.
#
# A card with no partition this script can mount is not a fault here: the
# program starts anyway, finds an empty bay, says so, and goes on watching ---
# and the CADR sees a controller with no drive on any of the eight units until
# a pack is copied in, at which point that unit's drive comes ready with no
# restart.
#
# The log goes to the console, which is where the board is watched from
# (docs/boot.md).  start-stop-daemon's -b would send it to /dev/null, so the
# program is told where to write instead.
#
# **AND THE CLOCK'S STEP LIVES HERE TOO, AND RUNS BEFORE EVERY OTHER ONE.**  No
# board here has a real-time clock in it, so `--date` and `--time` on the card
# are what tell this one the date, and `clock` beside them is what the last
# clean shutdown saved.  Both are on the partition this script mounts, which is
# the same reason the boot button's step is here, and the step is first because
# the machine, the programs and every file written want to agree from the first
# second.  `set_the_clock` below is the whole of it and `save_the_clock` is its
# other half, in `stop`.
#
# **AND THE BOOT BUTTON'S STEP LIVES HERE, WHICH NEEDS SAYING.**  `fpgarc`'s
# `--no-auto-boot`, and SW0 on the board, each leave the CADR's boot button
# unpressed, as a CADR is when the power comes on, and the machine is held with
# RUN clear until somebody presses it.  Two things fix where that step goes.  It must run BEFORE this
# script starts the pack program, because the drive coming present is what
# lets the boot PROM go on and read a band.  And it must read `fpgarc`, which
# is on the partition this script is the one thing that mounts.  A step of its
# own at S79 would therefore have to mount that partition itself, leaving the
# mount with two owners and one unmounter, so the step is here, between the
# mount and the drive.  `hold_the_machine` below is the whole of it, and it
# prefixes its one line `cadr-boot:` rather than `cadr-disk-packs:` because it
# is the boot button's and not the pack program's.
#
# **AND THE DEBUG CABLE'S ROLE AND ITS WIRING ARE HERE FOR THE SAME TWO
# REASONS.**
# `--debug-cable-connect` asks this board to be the debugger on Pmod JA, and it
# reaches the fabric through `cadr-console` exactly as the hold does.  It is
# read from `fpgarc`, which is on the partition this script mounts, and a step
# of its own would have to know that this one had mounted it.  It runs AFTER
# the hold and before the drive, which is an order and not an accident: a board
# that is going to debug somebody else should have taken the connector before
# its own machine starts asking a drive for a band.  `connect_the_debug_cable`
# below is the whole of it, and it prefixes its lines `cadr-debug-cable:`
# rather than `cadr-disk-packs:` for `hold_the_machine`'s reason.
#
# **THE WIRING IS SET BEFORE THE ROLE AND NOT AFTER**, which is an order and
# not a tidiness: the fabric refuses a wiring that moves under a board that is
# already the debugger, so a line that came second would be dropped and the
# card's setting would never reach the connector.

PROG=/usr/bin/cadr-disk-packs
CARD_DEV=/dev/mmcblk0p1
OLD_CARD_DEV=/dev/mmcblk0p2
CARD=/mnt/card
PIDFILE=/var/run/cadr-disk-packs.pid
# The drive bay, which is the one thing about the card that is not the same on
# both shapes, so it is a variable the mount below sets and every use reads.
# `packs/` on a card of the new shape; the whole of the second partition on a
# card of the old one, where the bay was that partition's root.
PACKS="$CARD/packs"
# **THE CARD'S PERMISSIONS, STATED BY THE MOUNT AND NOT BORROWED FROM WHOEVER
# RAN IT.**  FAT keeps no owner and no mode, so vfat invents them at mount
# time, and where the mount does not say, it takes the caller's umask.  At boot
# that is 022; from an ssh session it may be 077, and a card mounted by a
# restart from there hid `sys/` and `site/` from ozd, which runs as a user of
# its own.  So the mount names them: root owns everything and keeps write, and
# every user may read and search, which is what a boot has always given.
#
# **AND THE ozd GROUP MAY WRITE THE CARD.**  ozd serves the band's `site` tree
# read-write, so that a band can save the host table it generates there, and
# ozd refuses a writable root it cannot write.  So the card's group is ozd's
# and the group keeps write: `umask=0002`, which is rwx for root and the group
# and r-x for everybody else.  **THE COST, ACCEPTED: ozd CAN WRITE THE WHOLE
# CARD**, the packs, the boot files and `fpgarc` included, because FAT has no
# owner per directory and one mount gives every file the same group.  The
# group's id is looked up here at boot and not written in, because Buildroot
# picks it when it builds the image.  An image built without ozd has no such
# group, and its card is mounted with CARD_OPTS_ROOT, root's alone, as it
# always was.
#
# Everything else that writes the card runs as root --- this script's clock,
# the pack program, cadr-checkpoint, muir --- and root keeps write either way.
CARD_GROUP=ozd
CARD_OPTS_ROOT=rw,uid=0,gid=0,umask=0022
CARD_OPTS=$CARD_OPTS_ROOT

# The card's one file of flags, and cadr-common's reader.  `fpgarc` configures
# the CADR in the fabric the way `muirrc` beside it configures the CADR inside
# muir, and several programs take their own flags out of it, so the file goes
# through a filter and each program is handed the lines of the flags it owns.
# docs/fpgarc.md has every program's list; the reader's header is the
# contract.  This script keeps no list: it asks for each of its flags by name,
# and each function below says which.
RC="$CARD/fpgarc"
FPGARC_SH=/usr/share/cadr/fpgarc.sh
. "$FPGARC_SH"

# And the daemon starter, cadr-common's too: it starts the program, looks for
# it a moment later, and prints what it says when it is not there.  Its header
# is the whole account, and the short of it is that `start-stop-daemon -b`
# sends a refusal to /dev/null, so a flag this program will not take would
# otherwise be a boot that printed OK.
DAEMON_SH=/usr/share/cadr/daemon.sh
. "$DAEMON_SH"

# And the stopper, cadr-common's fourth file on the target: `start-stop-daemon
# -K` sends SIGTERM and returns at once, and `stop` below must not save the
# clock and unmount the card beside a program that is still writing to it.
# Its header is the whole account.
STOP_SH=/usr/share/cadr/stop.sh
. "$STOP_SH"

# **AND THE FAULT BITSTREAM**, cadr-common's fifth file on the target: whether
# the fabric is the one U-Boot loads when the CADR's cannot be loaded, which
# this program must not be started against.  `cadr_fault_stop` says so on the
# console once a boot and is asked first in `start`; its header is the whole
# account, and `docs/board.md` says what the board shows.
FAULT_SH=/usr/share/cadr/fault.sh
. "$FAULT_SH"

# **HOW LONG THE PACK PROGRAM IS GIVEN TO STOP: THIRTY SECONDS.**  On SIGTERM
# it finishes the pass it is in, writes back every dirty slot of the block
# store (`feeder_flush`, in up to 50 passes), takes the drives absent and
# closes the packs (cadr-disk-packs.c, the end of `main`).  The store has 24
# slots of one block each (PS_SLOTS in pack_side.h), and each write-back is a
# 1 KB `pwrite` and an `fdatasync` (pack_writeback in pack_file.c), so the
# worst case is 24 synchronous writes to the card, each of which on FAT may
# also write the FAT's own blocks.  The SD specification lets an SDHC or SDXC
# card hold a write busy for up to 500 ms, so 24 x 2 x 500 ms is 24 s at the
# very worst, and thirty is that with room.  An ordinary stop is far quicker:
# a slot is written back as soon as it is dirty, so there is seldom more than
# a few left.
PACKS_STOP_SECONDS=30

# And the clock, cadr-common's third file on the target.  No board here has a
# real-time clock in it, so `--date` and `--time` on the card are the only
# things that can tell this one what day it is, and what one clean shutdown
# saved is what the next boot starts from.  That file's header is the contract
# and `set_the_clock` below is the step.
CLOCK_SH=/usr/share/cadr/clock.sh
. "$CLOCK_SH"

# **WHERE THE CLOCK IS SAVED**, which is the card, beside the card's file of
# flags.  The card is the only place on this board that is both written and
# still there at the next boot, the root filesystem being a RAM disk unpacked
# at every boot.  One line, the fourteen digits `cadr_clock_now` prints.
CLOCK="$CARD/clock"

# **THE HOLD MARKER.**  It stands while the machine is held, and it is what
# `cadr-console` reads to refuse `start` and `step` with muir's own words and
# to remove when `boot` presses the button.  /var/run is on the root
# filesystem, which is a RAM disk unpacked at every boot, so a marker cannot
# in fact survive a reset --- it is removed below all the same, because a
# `restart` of this script is the case that can leave one standing and a
# marker that outlives its hold is a lie about the machine.
HELD=/var/run/cadr-held

# **THE MOUNT, AND THE ONE PLACE THAT KNOWS THERE WERE EVER TWO CARD SHAPES.**
# A card made by `mksd-*.sh` is one FAT32 partition holding everything, and
# that is the shape to build for.  A card of the OLD shape has two, with
# `fpgarc`, `muirrc`, `clock` and a flat drive bay on the second, and those
# cards are in boards that are running.  So the second partition is tried
# first, because a card that has one is old by definition, and the first is
# taken when there is no second.
#
# The order is what makes this safe.  Asking "is there a `packs/` folder?"
# would need the partition mounted before the question could be put, and
# mounting the wrong one first is what a board with a card half in its slot
# does.  Trying the second partition is one `mount` that either works or does
# not, and on a new card it does not exist at all.
#
# The bay's directory follows from which one answered, and it is what reaches
# the program as `--packs`.  Nothing else on the board differs: every other
# init script reads `$CARD/fpgarc`, which is the file either way.
mount_the_card() {
	mkdir -p "$CARD"
	# The ozd group's id, or nothing when the image has no ozd.  Only a
	# number is taken, so an `id` that printed words is the same as none.
	_card_gid=$(id -g "$CARD_GROUP" 2>/dev/null)
	case "$_card_gid" in
	''|*[!0-9]*) CARD_OPTS=$CARD_OPTS_ROOT; _card_gid="" ;;
	*) CARD_OPTS="rw,uid=0,gid=$_card_gid,umask=0002" ;;
	esac
	_card_mounted=now
	if mountpoint -q "$CARD" 2>/dev/null; then
		# Already mounted, which is what a `restart` that did not reach
		# its own `stop` leaves.  Here the question CAN be put by
		# looking, because the filesystem is there: a card with a
		# `packs/` folder is of the new shape and one without is not.
		[ -d "$CARD/packs" ] || PACKS="$CARD"
		_card_mounted=already
		echo "cadr-disk-packs: the card is already mounted at $CARD, and the drive bay is $PACKS"
	elif mount -t vfat -o "$CARD_OPTS" "$OLD_CARD_DEV" "$CARD" 2>/dev/null; then
		# A card of the old two-partition shape.  Its bay is the root of
		# this partition and not a folder in it.
		PACKS="$CARD"
		echo "cadr-disk-packs: THIS CARD IS OF THE OLD TWO-PARTITION SHAPE: $OLD_CARD_DEV is at $CARD"
		echo "cadr-disk-packs:   and its drive bay is that partition's own root.  It works, and it is"
		echo "cadr-disk-packs:   the last shape that will: format the card as ONE FAT32 partition and"
		echo "cadr-disk-packs:   unpack this board's zip onto it when you next have it in a reader."
	elif mount -t vfat -o "$CARD_OPTS" "$CARD_DEV" "$CARD" 2>/dev/null; then
		echo "cadr-disk-packs: the card is at $CARD, read-write, and the drive bay is $PACKS"
	else
		echo "cadr-disk-packs: no card at $CARD_DEV; the bay will be empty"
		_card_gid=none
	fi
	# What this mount gave the card, said only when this script mounted it:
	# a card that was already mounted keeps whatever its mount gave it.
	if [ "$_card_gid" = none ] || [ "$_card_mounted" = already ]; then
		:
	elif [ -n "$_card_gid" ]; then
		echo "cadr-disk-packs: the $CARD_GROUP group (gid $_card_gid) may write the card, so ozd can serve site/ read-write"
	else
		echo "cadr-disk-packs: there is no $CARD_GROUP user on this image, so the card is root's alone ($CARD_OPTS_ROOT)"
	fi
	mkdir -p "$PACKS" 2>/dev/null || true
}

start() {
	mount_the_card
	set_the_clock
	# **THE CARD AND THE CLOCK ARE NOT THE FABRIC'S**, so they are done on
	# the fault bitstream too: the card is where the configuration to be
	# checked is, and somebody fixing it over the network wants it mounted.
	# Everything after this asks the fabric something.
	cadr_fault_stop && return 0
	set_the_lamps
	set_the_display_boards
	set_the_display_output
	hold_the_machine
	connect_the_debug_cable
	# `--log` is not here and not the card's: `cadr_daemon` gives every
	# program the same two destinations, the console and a file under
	# /var/log that somebody over ssh can follow.
	#
	# **`--machine`, THE CARD'S, SHARED WITH THE SCREEN'S PROGRAM**: a card
	# carrying a QUUX bitstream says `--machine quux`, and QUUX's block-disk
	# asks for a block by its number where the CADR's controller asks by
	# cylinder, head and block.  Passed only when the card says it; the
	# program's default is the CADR.
	set --
	if [ -f "$RC" ] && fpgarc_has "$RC" --machine; then
		eval "set -- $(fpgarc_args "$RC" --machine)"
	fi
	cadr_daemon "$PROG" "$PIDFILE" --packs "$PACKS" "$@"
}

# **WHAT DAY IT IS, WHICH NOTHING ON THIS BOARD KNOWS.**  There is no
# real-time clock in it: `date` straight after a boot reads the epoch,
# /sys/class/rtc is empty and there is no /dev/rtc.  So a board that boots from
# its card alone is at the 1st of January 1970 until something tells it
# otherwise, and every file it writes says so.
#
# Two lines on the card tell it.  `--date yyyyMMdd` and `--time HHmm`, with the
# second after the minute if it is wanted.  The clock is UTC, which is what this
# board's is.
#
# **EACH LINE NAMES EXACTLY WHAT IT SETS AND THIS STEP GUESSES NOTHING ELSE.**
# `--date` alone sets the date and leaves the time of day where it stands;
# `--time` alone sets the time of day and leaves the date where it stands.  A
# lone `--time` is not that time today, because this board has no today: a date
# it invented would be a day nobody meant, which is the thing the card's whole
# file of flags exists to prevent.
#
# **AND WHAT THE LAST CLEAN SHUTDOWN SAVED IS RESTORED FIRST**, from `clock`
# beside the card's file of flags, so that a board that has been told the date
# once has a date for a lone `--time` to leave alone.  The restore is first and
# the card's lines are set on top of it.
#
# **THE TWO ARE NEVER COMPARED.**  A flag that is there overrides the field it
# names, whatever was restored and whatever the board came up with.  A rule
# that dropped a line because the saved clock happened to be later would be a
# value somebody wrote on the card and did not get, and it would be invisible:
# the card would say one thing and the board would do another.  If the clock
# has to be moved forward, the line on the card is what moves it.
#
# **IT IS FIRST, BEFORE EVERY OTHER STEP HERE AND BEFORE THE DRIVE COMES
# PRESENT**, because the machine, the programs and every file written want to
# agree from the first second --- a band read with the clock still at the epoch
# is a band whose files are stamped 1970 for ever.  And it is in THIS script
# for `hold_the_machine`'s two reasons: it reads the card's file of flags and
# the file it restores from, both of which are on the partition this script is
# the one thing that mounts.  The arithmetic under it --- which eight digits
# are a day that exists, which field of an instant a flag replaces --- is
# cadr-common's `clock.sh`, so that a check can try the wrong dates against it
# directly.
set_the_clock() {
	# The card's two lines.  A card with no fpgarc has neither.
	clock_date=
	clock_time=
	have_date=no
	have_time=no
	if [ -f "$RC" ]; then
		if fpgarc_has "$RC" --date; then
			have_date=yes
			eval "set -- $(fpgarc_args "$RC" --date)"
			clock_date=${2:-}
		fi
		if fpgarc_has "$RC" --time; then
			have_time=yes
			eval "set -- $(fpgarc_args "$RC" --time)"
			clock_time=${2:-}
		fi
	fi
	# Nothing said and nothing saved is a board whose clock nobody has ever
	# set, and it has nothing to say about it --- which is the rule every
	# other step in this script follows.
	[ "$have_date" = yes ] || [ "$have_time" = yes ] || [ -f "$CLOCK" ] ||
		return 0

	# What the board came up with, which with no clock in it is the epoch and
	# a few seconds of uptime.  It is the clock a flag's own field is set on
	# when there is nothing saved to set it on, so a clock that does not read
	# as fourteen digits is said and nothing is set: every line below rests on
	# this one being an instant.
	clock_now=$(cadr_clock_now)
	if ! cadr_clock_stamp_ok "$clock_now"; then
		echo "cadr-clock: the board's clock reads [$clock_now], which is not an" \
		     "instant, so nothing was set from $RC or from $CLOCK"
		return 0
	fi
	clock_want=$clock_now
	clock_from=

	# **WHAT THE LAST CLEAN SHUTDOWN SAVED, RESTORED FIRST AND WITHOUT A
	# CONDITION**, which is the clock this board had when somebody halted it.
	# It is restored so that the board has a date at all, which is what a lone
	# `--time` then leaves alone.  A file that is not fourteen digits is named
	# rather than guessed at: the partition is FAT32 and can be edited on a
	# laptop with a card reader, so a file with something else in it is a real
	# thing to meet.
	if [ -f "$CLOCK" ]; then
		clock_saved=$(cadr_clock_saved "$CLOCK")
		if ! cadr_clock_stamp_ok "$clock_saved"; then
			echo "cadr-clock: the clock saved in $CLOCK is [$clock_saved] and not the" \
			     "fourteen digits yyyyMMddHHmmss, so the clock was not restored from it"
		else
			clock_want=$clock_saved
			clock_from="restored from $CLOCK"
		fi
	fi

	# **AND THE CARD'S TWO LINES, EACH ON ITS OWN FIELD OF THE CLOCK AS IT NOW
	# STANDS**, which is the restored one where there was something to restore.
	# A flag that is there overrides the field it names and nothing else.  It
	# is not weighed against what was restored: `--time 0900` on a board halted
	# at two in the afternoon gives nine in the morning of that same day, which
	# is what the line asked for.
	clock_said=
	if [ "$have_date" = yes ]; then
		if cadr_clock_date_ok "$clock_date"; then
			clock_want=$(cadr_clock_with_date "$clock_want" "$clock_date")
			clock_said="--date $clock_date"
		else
			say_not_a_date "$clock_date"
		fi
	fi
	if [ "$have_time" = yes ]; then
		if cadr_clock_time_ok "$clock_time"; then
			clock_want=$(cadr_clock_with_time "$clock_want" "$clock_time")
			if [ -n "$clock_said" ]; then
				clock_said="$clock_said and --time $clock_time"
			else
				clock_said="--time $clock_time"
			fi
		else
			say_not_a_time "$clock_time"
		fi
	fi

	if [ -n "$clock_said" ]; then
		if [ -n "$clock_from" ]; then
			clock_from="$clock_said in $RC, on the clock $clock_from"
		else
			clock_from="$clock_said in $RC"
		fi
	fi

	# Nothing was restored and no line landed, so there is nothing to set and
	# nothing to say.
	[ -n "$clock_from" ] || return 0

	if cadr_clock_set "$clock_want"; then
		echo "cadr-clock: the clock is $(cadr_clock_human "$clock_want") UTC," \
		     "$clock_from"
	else
		echo "cadr-clock: the clock could not be set to" \
		     "$(cadr_clock_human "$clock_want") UTC; it is still" \
		     "$(cadr_clock_human "$clock_now") UTC"
	fi
}

# One line for a line on the card that is not the thing it says it is, with the
# form beside it: the person who wrote it has the card in a reader, and the
# form is the whole of what they need to put it right.  The boot goes on, which
# is what every other step here does with a setting it could not take.
say_not_a_date() {
	if [ -z "$1" ]; then
		echo "cadr-clock: --date is there with nothing after it, so the clock's date" \
		     "was not set from it: --date is yyyyMMdd, a four-digit year then a" \
		     "two-digit month 01 to 12 then a two-digit day the month has"
	else
		echo "cadr-clock: --date $1 is not a date, so the clock's date was not set" \
		     "from it: --date is yyyyMMdd, a four-digit year then a two-digit month" \
		     "01 to 12 then a two-digit day the month has"
	fi
}

say_not_a_time() {
	if [ -z "$1" ]; then
		echo "cadr-clock: --time is there with nothing after it, so the clock's time" \
		     "was not set from it: --time is HHmm or HHmmss, the hour 00 to 23 on a" \
		     "24-hour clock then the minute then the second, with no am and no pm"
	else
		echo "cadr-clock: --time $1 is not a time, so the clock's time was not set" \
		     "from it: --time is HHmm or HHmmss, the hour 00 to 23 on a 24-hour clock" \
		     "then the minute then the second, with no am and no pm"
	fi
}

# **THE CLOCK IS SAVED AT A CLEAN SHUTDOWN**, so that the next boot starts
# where this one left off rather than at the epoch.  It is written at this
# moment and no other: `stop` has waited for the pack program to exit, so
# nothing else is writing the partition, and the partition is about to be
# unmounted --- a save after that would write into the root filesystem's RAM
# disk, where the file would be there to find and gone at the next boot.  A
# pack program that did not exit in time is still writing its packs, which are
# other files; the clock is saved all the same.
#
# A board that lost its power instead of being halted keeps whatever the
# shutdown before it saved, which is the most a board with no clock in it can
# offer.
save_the_clock() {
	clock_stamp=$(cadr_clock_now)
	if ! cadr_clock_stamp_ok "$clock_stamp"; then
		echo "cadr-clock: the clock reads [$clock_stamp], which is not an instant" \
		     "to save; $CLOCK is left as it was"
		return 0
	fi
	# **THE `2>/dev/null` COMES FIRST BECAUSE REDIRECTIONS ARE APPLIED IN
	# ORDER**, which is the card reader's own lesson: written the other way
	# round the file is opened while stderr is still the console, so a
	# partition that is not writable prints the shell's own `cannot create`
	# and the `2>/dev/null` that was meant to catch it never sees it.
	if ! printf '%s\n' "$clock_stamp" 2>/dev/null > "$CLOCK"; then
		echo "cadr-clock: the clock could not be written to $CLOCK, so the next boot" \
		     "will start from whatever was last saved there"
		return 0
	fi
	echo "cadr-clock: $(cadr_clock_human "$clock_stamp") UTC saved to $CLOCK"
}

# **WHETHER THE BOARD'S ACTIVITY LAMPS BLINK.**  `--no-blinking-leds` makes
# them hold a level: on the Arty Z7-20 LD1 is then the clock generator's lock
# and LD2 is lit while the machine retires microcycles, and on the Cora Z7-07S
# LD1's green is the second of those.  What the lamps say does not change,
# only how, and the fabric comes up blinking.
#
# It is here because it reads `fpgarc`, which is on the partition this script
# is the one thing that mounts, and it is the FIRST thing this script asks the
# fabric for, because nothing waits on it and a person watching the board should
# see the lamps settle as early in the boot as anything can make them.  Only the
# clock is ahead of it, and nobody watches that.
#
# Nothing is printed when the flag is not there, because a board whose lamps
# blink has nothing to say about it.
set_the_lamps() {
	[ -f "$RC" ] && fpgarc_has "$RC" --no-blinking-leds || return 0
	if ! command -v cadr-console > /dev/null 2>&1; then
		echo "cadr-lamps: --no-blinking-leds, and no cadr-console to ask with;" \
		     "the lamps blink"
		return 0
	fi
	# `--log /dev/console` for the reason the debug cable's calls give: these
	# lines are a log and a line in a log names its program.
	# `blinking-leds off` exits 0 when the lamps are steady afterwards, so a
	# console that could not be reached and a fabric too old to have the word
	# both land here, and the console's own line has said which.
	if ! cadr-console --log /dev/console blinking-leds off 2>&1; then
		echo "cadr-lamps: --no-blinking-leds: the console did not make the lamps" \
		     "steady; they blink"
	fi
}

# **WHICH DISPLAY BOARDS THE BACKPLANE HAS**, and it is here for
# `hold_the_machine`'s two reasons: it reads `fpgarc`, which is on the
# partition this script is the one thing that mounts, and it must land before
# the drive comes present, because a machine that has read a band is a machine
# that may already have looked at a display board.
#
# `--tv-board` says which board the first display is, muir's own two words:
# MIT's SIMPLE TV, which System 100 drives, or the LISPM TV that replaced it in
# December 1980.  `--color-tv` fits the SECOND display board, the color TV at
# `0o17200000`.  Both are muir's flags with muir's meanings.
#
# **A MACHINE WITH NO COLOR BOARD MUST GIVE THE NXM AT THOSE ADDRESSES**,
# which is how `COLOR-EXISTS-P` in the band finds out whether it has one, so
# the board is off unless the card asks for it and a card that says nothing is
# a one-display machine --- which is muir's own default and the backplane every
# reference trace was taken on.
#
# **THE TWO ARE SET IN THIS ORDER AND THE ORDER DOES NOT MATTER**, which is
# worth saying because the debug cable's two lines below are an order and not a
# tidiness: these are two bits of one word and each key leaves the other alone,
# so a card carrying both gets both whichever way round they are read.
#
# Nothing is printed when neither flag is there, because a board with the
# backplane muir describes has nothing to say about it.
set_the_display_boards() {
	[ -f "$RC" ] || return 0
	fpgarc_has "$RC" --tv-board || fpgarc_has "$RC" --color-tv || return 0
	if ! command -v cadr-console > /dev/null 2>&1; then
		echo "cadr-display: --tv-board or --color-tv, and no cadr-console to ask with;" \
		     "the fabric's own backplane stands: one SIMPLE TV and no color TV"
		return 0
	fi
	# `--log /dev/console` for the reason the cable's calls give: these
	# lines are a log and a line in a log names its program.
	if fpgarc_has "$RC" --tv-board; then
		# One flag, one word, and the reader hands back both.  A
		# spelling the console does not know is refused BY the console,
		# which prints the two it takes.
		eval "set -- $(fpgarc_args "$RC" --tv-board)"
		if ! cadr-console --log /dev/console tv-board "${2:-simple-tv}" 2>&1; then
			echo "cadr-display: --tv-board ${2:-simple-tv}: cadr-console could not reach" \
			     "the console; the first board is whatever the fabric came up as"
		fi
	fi
	if fpgarc_has "$RC" --color-tv; then
		if ! cadr-console --log /dev/console color-tv on 2>&1; then
			echo "cadr-display: --color-tv: cadr-console could not reach the console;" \
			     "this machine has no color TV"
		fi
	fi
}

# **WHAT THE BOARD'S OWN DISPLAY OUTPUT SHOWS, AND WHICH WAY UP.**  Here for
# `set_the_display_boards`'s reasons, and read from the same file: the display
# output scans the display's region of DDR at a monitor's rate with no software
# in the path, so what it shows is the fabric's business and the card's, not a
# program's.
#
# `--hdmi-output` names the screens: the first display, the color board, or
# both --- at 1:1, the first display to the left and the color board to the
# right, with the color one drawn over the first where they share a column.
# `--hdmi-rotate` turns the picture a quarter turn for a monitor stood on its
# side, which is what a screen 768 by 963 wants.
#
# **`--hdmi-sleep` IS HOW LONG THE MONITOR WAITS**, in seconds, with nobody at
# the board's own keyboard or mouse, before the display output stops the link and
# the monitor sleeps --- a digital link has no other way to sleep one.  A key or
# the mouse at the board wakes it and a viewer's keys do not; zero never sleeps.
# The fabric comes up with three hundred, so a card that says nothing has that.
# `cadr-console hdmi-sleep` writes it at any time, and a board with no display
# output says so there rather than here.
#
# Nothing is printed when none of the three is there, because a board showing
# the machine's own screen upright and sleeping after the fabric's own three
# hundred seconds has nothing to say about it.
set_the_display_output() {
	[ -f "$RC" ] || return 0
	fpgarc_has "$RC" --hdmi-output || fpgarc_has "$RC" --hdmi-rotate ||
		fpgarc_has "$RC" --hdmi-sleep || return 0
	if ! command -v cadr-console > /dev/null 2>&1; then
		echo "cadr-display: --hdmi-output, --hdmi-rotate or --hdmi-sleep, and" \
		     "no cadr-console to ask with; the monitor shows the machine's own screen" \
		     "upright and sleeps after the fabric's own 300 seconds"
		return 0
	fi
	if fpgarc_has "$RC" --hdmi-output; then
		eval "set -- $(fpgarc_args "$RC" --hdmi-output)"
		if ! cadr-console --log /dev/console hdmi-output "${2:-tv}" 2>&1; then
			echo "cadr-display: --hdmi-output ${2:-tv}: cadr-console could not reach" \
			     "the console; the monitor shows whatever the fabric came up with"
		fi
	fi
	if fpgarc_has "$RC" --hdmi-rotate; then
		eval "set -- $(fpgarc_args "$RC" --hdmi-rotate)"
		if ! cadr-console --log /dev/console hdmi-rotate "${2:-0}" 2>&1; then
			echo "cadr-display: --hdmi-rotate ${2:-0}: cadr-console could not reach" \
			     "the console; the picture is whichever way up the fabric came up"
		fi
	fi
	if fpgarc_has "$RC" --hdmi-sleep; then
		# One flag and one number of seconds.  A number the console will not
		# take, and a board with no display output to hold it, are both
		# refused BY the console, whose line says which.
		eval "set -- $(fpgarc_args "$RC" --hdmi-sleep)"
		if ! cadr-console --log /dev/console hdmi-sleep "${2:-300}" 2>&1; then
			echo "cadr-display: --hdmi-sleep ${2:-300}: not set --- the console's line" \
			     "above says why"
		fi
	fi
}

# **LEAVE THE BOOT BUTTON UNPRESSED, AND THERE ARE TWO WAYS TO ASK FOR IT.**
# muir's `--no-auto-boot` leaves a CADR as it is when the power comes on: RUN
# clear, nothing run, and only the button starts it.  On this board that is
# asked for by the card's flag or by the switch, and the two are an OR --- a
# flag can never turn a switch off.
#
#   SW0 on the board     THE FABRIC has already done it.  The machine came out
#                        of reset with RUN clear and has never run a
#                        microcycle, so there is nothing here to halt; what
#                        this step does is leave the marker, so that
#                        cadr-console refuses start and step and says why.
#                        `cadr-console switch` exits 0 when the switch held it.
#   --no-auto-boot in    NOTHING in the fabric changes for it, so the machine
#   fpgarc               is held by halting it, and this is the last moment
#                        before the drive comes present, which is what a held
#                        machine must not see.
#
# **THE PROM HAS ALREADY RUN WHEN THE FLAG IS WHAT HOLDS IT, AND THAT IS NOT A
# GAP IN THE HOLD.**  The CADR starts when the bitstream is loaded, seconds
# before Linux reaches this script, and within a few hundred milliseconds it
# has cleared its control store and is in AWAIT-DRIVE-READY.  It can go no
# further, because no drive is present until the program below starts.  So the
# machine this halts is one that has done its PROM work and is waiting, with
# nothing of a band loaded; `cadr-console boot` presses the button and the PROM
# starts again from zero.  **The switch has no such gap**: the machine never
# ran at all.
#
# Nothing is printed when neither asks for it, because a board that boots
# itself has nothing to say about it.
hold_the_machine() {
	rm -f "$HELD"
	# The card's flag.  A card with no fpgarc has no flag.
	no_auto_boot_flag=no
	if [ -f "$RC" ] && fpgarc_has "$RC" --no-auto-boot; then
		no_auto_boot_flag=yes
	fi
	# And the switch, asked of the fabric itself rather than of any file.
	# A console that cannot be reached answers no, which is the safe
	# direction: it is also the console that could not have halted anything.
	no_auto_boot_switch=no
	if command -v cadr-console > /dev/null 2>&1 &&
	   cadr-console switch > /dev/null 2>&1; then
		no_auto_boot_switch=yes
	fi
	[ "$no_auto_boot_flag" = yes ] || [ "$no_auto_boot_switch" = yes ] || return 0

	if [ "$no_auto_boot_switch" = yes ]; then
		# Already held, by the fabric, since the reset.  No halt is
		# issued: there is nothing running to halt, and a halt here
		# would be this script taking credit for the switch's work.
		echo "held at boot by SW0" > "$HELD"
		if [ "$no_auto_boot_flag" = yes ]; then
			echo "cadr-boot: SW0: the machine came up with RUN clear and has never run;" \
			     "--no-auto-boot in $RC asks for the same thing"
		else
			echo "cadr-boot: SW0: the machine came up with RUN clear and has never run"
		fi
	else
		if ! command -v cadr-console > /dev/null 2>&1; then
			echo "cadr-boot: --no-auto-boot, and no cadr-console to halt the machine with;" \
			     "it is running"
			return 0
		fi
		if ! cadr-console halt > /dev/null 2>&1; then
			echo "cadr-boot: --no-auto-boot, and cadr-console could not halt the machine;" \
			     "it is running"
			return 0
		fi
		echo "held at boot by --no-auto-boot in $RC" > "$HELD"
		echo "cadr-boot: --no-auto-boot: the machine is held with RUN clear"
	fi
	echo "cadr-boot: \`cadr-console boot\` or BTN0 on the board presses the boot button"
}

# **WHICH WAY ROUND THE JA RIBBON WAS MADE.**  A Pmod cable is supposed to join
# pin one to pin one.  One made from two host sockets mirrors the header's two
# rows instead, so each board's pins 1 to 4 reach the other's 7 to 10, and a
# debugger then drives four pins the far board never listens to.  Two boards
# were found on exactly such a cable.
#
# `auto` is the fabric's own default and looks for the answer, so a card that
# says nothing is a board that finds out for itself; `straight` and `crossover`
# take the looking out of the way when somebody is diagnosing a cable.  Only a
# DEBUGGER applies the setting, so it changes nothing on a board that is a
# debuggee --- but it is set anyway and before the role, because the fabric
# refuses a wiring that moves under a board that already has the role.
#
# Nothing is printed when the flag is not there: a board on `auto` has nothing
# to say about it until it takes the role.
set_the_debug_cable_wiring() {
	[ -f "$RC" ] && fpgarc_has "$RC" --debug-cable-wiring || return 0
	if ! command -v cadr-console > /dev/null 2>&1; then
		echo "cadr-debug-cable: --debug-cable-wiring, and no cadr-console to ask with;" \
		     "the fabric's own default stands"
		return 0
	fi
	# One flag, one value, and the reader hands back both words.  A spelling
	# the console does not know is refused BY the console, which prints the
	# three it takes.
	eval "set -- $(fpgarc_args "$RC" --debug-cable-wiring)"
	# **`--log /dev/console` AND NOT JUST THE SCRIPT'S OWN OUTPUT.**  The
	# console writes a bare reply to a person at a terminal and a line
	# naming its program to a log, and `/dev/console` IS a terminal, so a
	# line let through from here would go into the boot log with nothing
	# saying which of six programs said it.  The flag is this script saying
	# that these lines are a log.
	if ! cadr-console --log /dev/console debug-cable-wiring "${2:-auto}" 2>&1; then
		echo "cadr-debug-cable: --debug-cable-wiring ${2:-auto}: cadr-console could not" \
		     "reach the console; the fabric's own default stands"
	fi
}

# **THE DEBUG CABLE'S ROLE ON PMOD JA.**  A board with nothing said is a
# DEBUGGEE: it answers a debugger that plugs into the connector, exactly as
# MIT's board answers one on its DBGIN, and nothing has to be set for it.
# `--debug-cable-connect` asks for the other role, and it is muir's own flag
# with muir's own meaning.  There is no listen flag here or there, because
# listening is what a CADR always does.
#
# **ASKING IS NOT HAVING, so the line says what happened and not what was
# asked.**  A board that can see a debugger already driving the connector
# refuses, and the first board told is the one that has it.  `cadr-console
# debug-cable-connect` reads the role back after the write and prints it, so
# what reaches the console at boot is the outcome.
#
# Nothing is printed when the flag is not there, because a board that is a
# debuggee has nothing to say about it.
connect_the_debug_cable() {
	# **QUUX HAS NO DEBUG CABLE** (contract Q5): the cable is a Unibus
	# master, and QUUX has no Unibus, as muir refuses the cable's flags on
	# QUUX.  A card that says `--machine quux` has either line refused here.
	if fpgarc_is_quux; then
		if fpgarc_has "$RC" --debug-cable-connect || fpgarc_has "$RC" --debug-cable-wiring; then
			echo "cadr-debug-cable: --machine quux: QUUX has no Unibus, and so no debug cable;" \
			     "the card's debug cable lines are refused"
		fi
		return 0
	fi
	set_the_debug_cable_wiring
	[ -f "$RC" ] && fpgarc_has "$RC" --debug-cable-connect || return 0
	if ! command -v cadr-console > /dev/null 2>&1; then
		echo "cadr-debug-cable: --debug-cable-connect, and no cadr-console to ask with;" \
		     "this board is still a debuggee"
		return 0
	fi
	# `--log /dev/console` for the reason the wiring's call gives: these
	# lines are a log and a line in a log names its program.
	if ! cadr-console --log /dev/console debug-cable-connect 2>&1; then
		echo "cadr-debug-cable: --debug-cable-connect: cadr-console could not reach the" \
		     "console; this board is still a debuggee"
	fi
}

# **THE ORDER IS THE WHOLE OF IT**: the pack program gone, then the clock
# saved, then everything synced, then the card unmounted.  `cadr_stop` waits
# for the program, because it is writing the machine's last blocks back as it
# leaves.  A program still there at the bound is said and left running rather
# than killed, since a kill would lose exactly those blocks; the clock is still
# saved, and the unmount then fails because the packs are open, which is said
# too.  A card that is not mounted --- a board with no card in it --- has
# nothing to unmount and nothing is said.
stop() {
	cadr_stop cadr-disk-packs "$PIDFILE" "$PACKS_STOP_SECONDS"
	save_the_clock
	sync
	# One mount, so one unmount.  The clock is saved above it, which is the
	# order that matters: a save after the unmount writes into the root
	# filesystem's RAM disk and is gone at the next boot, with the file
	# there to find either way.
	mountpoint -q "$CARD" 2>/dev/null || return 0
	if ! _unmount_said=$(umount "$CARD" 2>&1); then
		echo "cadr-disk-packs: $CARD WAS NOT UNMOUNTED, so what is on the card may not" \
		     "all be written: ${_unmount_said:-umount said nothing}"
		return 1
	fi
	return 0
}

case "$1" in
	start) start ;;
	stop) stop ;;
	restart|reload) stop; start ;;
	*) echo "Usage: $0 {start|stop|restart}"; exit 1 ;;
esac

exit $?
